# chezmoi remove — target paths (relative to $HOME) that chezmoi must delete.

# On macOS, tools built on os.UserConfigDir() resolve a ~/Library path ahead of
# ~/.config whenever XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset. A file left at the higher-
# precedence path wins silently: the managed ~/.config copy is never read, and
# because the shadowing file is not in the chezmoi source, `chezmoi status` and
# `chezmoi diff` report nothing wrong.
#
# Config only. lazygit's state.yml stays — it is state, not config, and deleting
# it would discard recent-repo history on every apply.
#
# lazygit only: once no config file remains at the ~/Library path, lazygit falls
# back to ~/.config/lazygit and never re-creates the file, so the removal is a
# permanent fix.
#
# glow is deliberately NOT listed. Its default path stays under ~/Library whether
# or not a file is there, so removal never redirects it to ~/.config — and glow
# *writes its own default config* at that path on any invocation without
# XDG_CONFIG_HOME. That makes the removal both ineffective and destructive: after
# glow re-creates the file, `chezmoi apply` fails with "has changed since chezmoi
# last wrote it" and refuses to continue until the file is deleted by hand.
# What actually guarantees glow reads the managed ~/.config/glow/glow.yml is
# dot_zshrc.tmpl exporting XDG_CONFIG_HOME=$HOME/.config, which covers every real
# invocation (the fzf previews, `mdview` from lazygit).
{{- if eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" }}
Library/Application Support/lazygit/config.yml
{{- end }}

# Dead on every platform: dot_gitconfig.tmpl sets core.excludesFile, which
# replaces this XDG default rather than stacking with it. Its one rule now lives
# in dot_gitignore_global.
.config/git/ignore

